     Okay, first, all this quoted text is Hairston's.  I forgot to point
that out last time.  (Except, of course, things I wrote, which should be
marked with a [G].)
 
:[G] speed and taking out enough features (on the PC version) to eliminate
:[G] overlay rights, because as is, there's no way in HELL I could possibly
:[G] avoid making those mistakes occasionally.
:
:seems to me that you _are_ seriously suggesting that the machine is the
:reason for your poor play. perhaps you should make long moves using <ctrl>
:keys so you'll stop when something interesting appears instead of typing
:ahead movement keys. hmmm ...
     Okay:
1. In most cases, I do use shift- keys to move; I'm not sure whether I
*can* use control keys with the numbered keypad, but I don't believe I can.
For some reason unknown to me, Ctrl-up and Ctrl-down DO NOT yield a ROM-BIOS
response on the IBM, so Nethack would have to have its own keyboard driver.
I don't believe it does that, so at best I'd only be able to use Ctrl-s on
the other six directions.
     I could switch back to letters (or use ANSI sequences to redefine my
number keys to those letters and use the letter-keys interface).
     It's an idea.  How exactly do the Ctrl-keys operate?  Because in most
of the cases I'm talking about, the shift-keys would be inappropriate to
use in the case; for instance, they carry me right past intersections.  Thus
when moving to an intersection, even if it's quite a way down the hall, I
must use single moves.
 
:by the way, it is a thinking game. playing with reckless abandon will
:tend to be frustating.
     Don't get me wrong.  I haven't gotten past level 5 (I could, but in
my experience with just level five I'd die quite rapidly) in the week or
two that I've played pl7.  My character is generally level 9 before I even
venture onto level 9.  I spend a lot of time making up *strategy.*  I
CERTAINLY don't play with "reckless abandon."
     [March 14: "My character is generally level 9 before I even venture
onto level 5" rather... typo.]
 
:well, you're right! i haven't played on a pc. is it really a dog? or is
:just that with your superior typing skills it seems like a dog?
     I don't type fast.  The pc version is just fine as far as playing
enjoyment goes; it only does disk reads occasionally.  I also should have
qualified; I'm running on an 8-bit, 10MHz machine with a hard drive that
could really use a new physical format (the interleave factor needs
changing; I don't want to get technical).
     I don't type fast while playing the game, rather.  I rarely run into
things when the game ISN'T doing a read.  But when it does to a read, I almost
always accidentally slip in an extra command or to.  Were I to ensure that I
never did this, I'd have to wait two or three seconds in-between keystrokes
(the disk reads don't often take that long, but sometimes they do).
     The game likes to do the disk reads most often when there are
monsters nearby, in my experience.  Thus I'm most likely to fit those extra
commands in in time to do the exact wrong thing (via Murphy's law) in regard
to a monster I couldn't see before.  This isn't so bad normally, and can't
really be corrected, UNLESS we get these one-step-death formulas like the
floating eye.
     I wouldn't complain if I had actually intended to hit the floating
eyes.  But in most of these cases I had actually been taking pains NOT to
be frozen (for instance, I mentioned one case where I had a wand of make
invisible.)
:are you suggesting that nymphs never steal from females because that is
:somehow unrealistic??? my understanding is that nymphs steal! they do
:this to males by seducing them and to females by charming them. what's
     That they steal from females (or males) isn't unrealistic as far as
personality.  That they can do so automatically no matter what the male
or female in question is trying to do to prevent them from doing so is
unrealistic.
     A nymph seducing a female is pretty rediculous.  Changing it to a faun
wouldn't do anything to play balance.  I can deal with nymphs, most of the
time.  (Admittedly, occasionally they have a knack for stealing just the
object I wanted to have most at the time, but not often.)  But that they
can steal my +4 axe named Cleavery that I was *wielding* (I didn't know
nymphs were known for their strength; they'd have to tug awfully hard
at that thing to have any greater chance of wrenching it out of my hand
than, say, having it strike a hard object like some monster's head), in
one turn, without seeming to have any problems with hitting through a
-3 AC, doesn't seem realistic.
     (My first two sentences meant that I didn't mind all that much that
they can seduce females; sorry if I gave the wrong impression.)
     IThe only explanation I can divine for them having such ease is that
the characters aren't willing to prevent them.  Yet I'm quite good at
throwing darts at nymphs or striking them with sharp objects.
 
:version a dog? or is it just you? (in terms of speed of response)
     The speed of response is ORDINARILY fine.  And, on a lot of systems,
I'm sure it's fine enough of the time (Kevin Smolkowski tells me that
he only gets reads on level changes; I have all my TSRs cleaned out and
the same 640K of maximum adressable memory, but I may be using a version of
MS-DOS that eats up more memory than his) that floating eyes aren't a
problem.  But sometime in the two or three hours that the start of a game
can often take, I'm liable to make just ONE slip, especially on my system
which is a bit slow in both processor and drive, and with overlay reads
every so often (again, especially with a monster nearby).
     BTW, I'd like to congratulate the dev team for a great job on pl7.
Patchlevel 6 *was* slow-- slow enough so as to be unplayable on my
system.  (It worked well enough on a friend's system with a ramdrive,
however; I may be upgrading to some more memory soon, which would also
alleviate this problem.)  Pl7 is *much* better; the overlay reads are
shorter and happen less often.
---------
     Okay, to sum:
Nymphs: Don't change much.  I find it unrealistic, but I was just saying
that.  They don't hurt game balance enough to be annoying.
 
I'll let Richard clarify what he said; I understood it well enough, but
I don't want to get redundant.
 
Floating eyes: I only want a single, not very difficult, change: that there
be an option, sort of like the one we have for not attacking pets and
shopkeepers (or is that still an option?) that would confirm whether you
wanted to attack floating eyes for the FIRST time.  (i.e. keep asking you,
and if you every say yes and actually attack, stop asking).  Perhaps
green molds and acid blobs too, but still just one option if so.  YOU
wouldn't have to enable the option, and *I* wouldn't have any special
advantage; if my strategy includes bashing floating eyes and acid blobs,
I'll simply answer yes to the message, freeze myself, and die.  However,
it doesn't; it's the occasional overlay read that sends me hurtling into
such monsters as acid blobs and (much more annoyingly) floating eyes.
     I don't recommend that every single move that might cause one
harm be confirmed.  I don't recommend that we reconfirm anything.  Just
one or two cases where people often make moves they never intended to do.
 
:i've been told that the pc version is _slow_ [from Hairston, again]
     Not from me.  It's *irregular* on some systems.
 
:it's also clear that typing
:ahead is a mistake.
     And it's also clear that it wasn't intended.
 
:then complain that the game needs fixing ...
     The game could use some improvements, like any software package.
A "thinking game" doesn't involve spending a few seconds in between moves
going down a corridor because you're afraid the game might pull a pause
for an overlay read at some point and you'll run into a floating eye.
 
:(remember we can't single out eyes and blobs, that's
:idiosyncratic)???
     Of course we can!  I don't lose anything for hitting a kobold that
I was likely to hit anway (if I'm at really low hp anyway, I'm generally
typing really slow to begin with, because I need more time to think.  I
obviously can't play at that speed when simply going to a level's staircase
unless I've got about six hours a day free time.)  Acid blobs and gren fungi
I don't care about all that much.  It's floating eyes, where if you hit
them ONCE at full hp, you're likely to get frozen and subsequently butchered
by a cave spider, that are the ONLY problem the extra keys result in.
 
:discipline richard and greg need to learn.
     I thought I'd made it clear in my first message to you that such
discipline is impossible due to the overlay reads I get, often right next
to a floating eye.
 
:i have heard that the game does not tolerate sleepy/sloppy play.
     I don't play when sleepy myself.  *I'm*, in fact, taking a lot of
measures NOT to make such mistakes, most of the time.
     [Obviously, I only post articles when I'm asleep; I save my 
waking hours for nethack.  -sigh-]

